---
// Rewritten as a first-person founder letter (issue #129). The sweep's verdict
// on the old page: "clear positioning but anonymous — for a solo-founder
// product selling trust to skeptical contractors, a first-person founder note
// would outperform this corporate voice." Voice and personal details reviewed
// by Ivan before merge; the positioning content below carries over from the
// original page.
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Header from '../components/Header.astro';
import CTA from '../components/CTA.astro';
import Footer from '../components/Footer.astro';
---

<Layout
  title="About BuildWorkPro – Built by a Contractor Who Needed It"
  description="BuildWorkPro was built inside a working glazing contractor by its founder — subcontractor-first software at one flat price, tested on real jobs first."
>
  <Header />
  <main class="pt-32 pb-20">
    <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8">
      <div class="text-center mb-16">
        <p class="text-sm font-semibold text-brand-600 uppercase tracking-wider mb-3">About</p>
        <h1 class="text-4xl sm:text-5xl font-extrabold text-slate-900 tracking-tight text-balance">
          Built by a contractor who needed it
        </h1>
        <p class="mt-6 text-lg text-slate-500 max-w-2xl mx-auto leading-relaxed">
          BuildWorkPro didn't start in a pitch deck. It started in a glass shop in South Florida,
          with a stack of bids to get out and software quotes that made no sense.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-slate mx-auto">
        <p>
          I run <a href="/customers/national-glass/">National Glass and Construction</a> — a glass and
          glazing contractor. Storefronts, impact windows and doors, showers, railings. The same week-in,
          week-out loop every specialty sub knows: price the work, win the work, schedule the crew, document
          the field, bill the work, chase the payment.
        </p>
        <p>
          When I went looking for software to run that loop, the industry's answer was software
          built for the other side of the table — general contractors and owners — at prices
          designed for them too: per-user fees that punish you for hiring, custom quotes that go up
          every renewal, and the features subs actually live in — pay applications, change orders,
          field logs — locked behind enterprise tiers. The affordable options stopped at invoices.
        </p>
        <p>So I built the tool I couldn't buy, and ran my own company on it.</p>

        <h2>Every feature ran real jobs first</h2>
        <p>
          National Glass has put <a href="/customers/national-glass/">416 bids and 143 projects</a> through
          BuildWorkPro. When a bid takes one click too many or a pay application rounds wrong, it costs
          my crew before it ever reaches yours. That's the feedback loop this product is built on — contractor
          first, software second.
        </p>

        <h2>What we stand for</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>Subs first.</strong> Every feature decision starts with "does this help a specialty
            contractor?" If it doesn't, we don't build it.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>One price, everything included.</strong> $79/month flat. The plumber with a 5-person
            crew deserves the same tools as the GC with a $100M backlog — and hiring your sixth person
            should cost you $0 more.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Built to be used.</strong> Deep on the eight things a sub does every week, instead
            of shallow on eighty things nobody asked for.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Your data, your business.</strong> Full export, any time. We sell software, not your
            data — and leaving is never a hostage negotiation.
          </li>
        </ul>

        <p>
          If that sounds like how you'd want your software company run, the
          <a href="/pricing/">whole platform is $79/month</a> and the first 14 days are free — no credit
          card, no demo call, no sales rep. Try it on a real job and see.
        </p>

        <p class="not-prose mt-10 text-slate-700">
          — <strong>Ivan</strong><br />
          <span class="text-sm text-slate-500"
            >Founder, BuildWorkPro · Owner, National Glass and Construction</span
          >
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="mt-16">
      <CTA />
    </div>
  </main>
  <Footer />
</Layout>
